From f87657ac882c920e82b6942b9dd158107bbe2877 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: l4h <z@univalence.me>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 08:32:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] mcs: add per-TCB deadline timers (seL4_TCB_SetTimer)

Allow user level to arm a one-shot, absolute deadline timer that signals a
badge to the TCB's bound notification when it expires. Armed timers are
kept in a per-core red-black tree ordered by deadline, and its first node
is taken into account when programming the MCS kernel's one-shot timer, so
no additional hardware timer is needed.

A timer is never signalled before its deadline, and the hardware is
programmed for the deadline itself, not one timer precision earlier as
for budget and release events (whose checks are exact: an interrupt that
comes too early for them is raised again at once).  Signalling a
precision early let a user that checks the time on wakeup see no expired
timer and wait again, with nothing armed.  Because timers fire from the
scheduler, the thread signalled may have blocked in the same kernel entry
and still hold the current scheduling context: sendSignal() already skips
the refill check then, and no longer asserts that it cannot happen.

User level chooses how many timers are armed: any TCB it can retype can
hold one. Arming and disarming therefore take O(log n) time, and at most
32 timers fire per kernel entry; due timers beyond those fire on the next
timer interrupt, which is programmed to come at once. A sorted list let
one user arm 100,000 timers and then hold the kernel lock for milliseconds
on each SetTimer.

This gives user-level OS personalities (e.g. paravirtualised guest
kernels) a cheap clock-event device on platforms without a spare timer
device such as cloud VMs without HPET.
